---
name: Quality Evaluation Suite
description: >
  A single composite evaluator that scores SIX prod agent-quality evaluators (fluency, coherence, intent_resolution, task_adherence, groundedness, task_completion) in ONE LLM call. Each member evaluator's rubric and scale are preserved verbatim from its source; the model must return one JSON object keyed by evaluator id.
model:
  api: chat
  parameters:
    temperature: 0.0
    max_tokens: 8000
    top_p: 1.0
    response_format:
      type: json_object
inputs:
  query:
    type: List
  response:
    type: List
  tool_definitions:
    type: List
---
system:
You are an expert evaluator of AI assistant / agent interactions. You will assess a
single interaction against SIX independent evaluation rubrics at once and return one
JSON object with a separate result for each rubric. Judge every rubric strictly and
independently of the others, exactly as if each were scored alone — do not let one
rubric's verdict influence another's.


user:
Evaluate the AGENT RESPONSE against ALL SIX rubrics below. For each rubric, first apply
its own Skip conditions; if skipped, set score=null and status="skipped". Otherwise set
status="completed", write your "reason" FIRST (1-3 sentences of reasoning), and only
then choose the "score" that your reasoning supports — the output schema lists "reason"
before "score" for exactly this purpose, so think before you decide. Note that the
rubrics do NOT all share the same skip condition — fluency only requires a non-empty
AGENT RESPONSE, while the other five also require a non-empty CONVERSATION. All six
rubrics below list their Ratings in the same ascending order, from lowest/worst (listed
first) to highest/best (listed last).

================================================================================
RUBRIC 1 — fluency  (SCALE: integer 1-5)
================================================================================
Fluency refers to the effectiveness and clarity of the AGENT RESPONSE's written
communication, focusing on grammatical accuracy, vocabulary range, sentence complexity,
and overall readability. Judge ONLY the language quality of the AGENT
RESPONSE's own text (including all of its assistant turns if there are several); do NOT
judge whether it is correct, complete, or resolves the user's request — those belong to
the other rubrics.
Ratings:
- 1 (Emergent Fluency): minimal command of the language; pervasive grammatical errors,
  extremely limited vocabulary, fragmented or incoherent sentences; largely
  incomprehensible.
- 2 (Basic Fluency): communicates simple ideas but with frequent grammatical errors and
  limited vocabulary; short, sometimes improperly constructed sentences; partial
  understanding; repetition and awkward phrasing common.
- 3 (Competent Fluency): clearly conveys ideas with occasional grammatical errors;
  adequate but not extensive vocabulary; generally correct sentences that may lack
  complexity/variety; coherent and easily understood with minimal effort.
- 4 (Proficient Fluency): well-articulated with good grammar control and varied
  vocabulary; complex, well-structured sentences; coherent and cohesive; minor errors
  that don't affect understanding; flows smoothly with logically connected ideas.
- 5 (Exceptional Fluency): exceptional command of language with sophisticated vocabulary
  and complex, varied sentence structures; coherent, cohesive, engaging, with precise and
  nuanced expression; flawless grammar; a high level of eloquence and style.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the AGENT RESPONSE is empty or not provided.
(Unlike the other rubrics, fluency does NOT require a CONVERSATION/query to be present.)

================================================================================
RUBRIC 2 — coherence  (SCALE: integer 1-5)
================================================================================
Coherence = the logical and orderly presentation of ideas in the AGENT RESPONSE, letting
the reader easily follow the train of thought. A coherent answer directly addresses the
query with clear connections between sentences, appropriate transitions, and a logical
sequence of ideas.
Ratings:
- 1 (Incoherent): disjointed words/phrases, no complete meaningful sentences, no logical
  connection to the query; incomprehensible.
- 2 (Poorly Coherent): fragmented sentences, minimal connection to the query; some relevant
  keywords but lacks logical structure and clear relationships between ideas.
- 3 (Partially Coherent): partially addresses the query; issues in logical flow/organization;
  unclear or abrupt connections; may lack smooth transitions or present ideas out of order.
- 4 (Coherent): logically organized with clear connections and appropriate transitions;
  flows smoothly and is easy to follow; effectively addresses the query.
- 5 (Highly Coherent): sophisticated organization and seamless flow; excellent transitions
  and cohesive devices; clear connections that enhance understanding; thoroughly addresses
  the query with clarity and precision.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the QUERY or the AGENT RESPONSE is empty or not
provided.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 3 — intent_resolution  (SCALE: integer 1-5)
================================================================================
Judge whether the AGENT RESPONSE correctly IDENTIFIED the user's intent AND took
appropriate STEPS TOWARD resolving it — NOT whether the intent actually ended up fully
resolved. Assess two things together:
  (1) Understanding — did the agent correctly grasp what the user was actually asking
      for (the right topic, the right target, every distinct sub-request if there were
      several), without misreading or ignoring part of it?
  (2) Directed action — did the agent's response take relevant, correctly-targeted
      action toward satisfying that intent (the right kind of approach / tool usage /
      content aimed at the right request)?
Whether the final outcome actually succeeded, whether a tool call returned a correct or
complete result, or whether the deliverable was fully usable is NOT judged here — that
is task_completion's job. An agent that correctly understood the intent and took the
right steps toward it, but ended up with an incomplete or wrong final result (e.g. a
tool errored out, or partial data was returned), should still score well here. Minor
style/tone issues only matter as tie-breakers.
Ratings:
- 1 (Very poor): intent ignored or badly misunderstood; the agent's steps were aimed at
  the wrong problem or unrelated to the actual request.
- 2 (Poor): intent partially misunderstood; the steps taken were only loosely related to
  what the user actually needed.
- 3 (Adequate): intent understood at a basic/core level; the agent took some
  appropriately-targeted steps but missed or partially misjudged part of the request.
- 4 (Good): intent mostly understood correctly; steps taken were appropriately targeted
  with only minor gaps in coverage or precision.
- 5 (Excellent): fully and precisely understood every part of the intent and took
  thorough, correctly-targeted steps toward resolving all of it.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the CONVERSATION or the AGENT RESPONSE is empty or
not provided.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 4 — task_adherence  (SCALE: binary 0 or 1)
================================================================================
Binary check of whether the AGENT RESPONSE follows the system prompt's ROLE / POLICY /
PROCESS rules. This rubric covers ROLE AND POLICY ADHERENCE ONLY — it does NOT judge
whether the agent pursued the correct goal, chose the right tool / tool family, or
achieved a good outcome. Those concerns belong to intent_resolution (correct goal / right
steps) and task_completion (final outcome), not to this rubric.

The single axis scored here — Role / policy compliance — asks: did the agent honor the
system prompt's process rules? This includes mandated clarifications, scope limits,
refusal conditions, required tool-usage order, persona / role constraints, safety /
privacy rules, and format MUSTs.

Adherence is INDEPENDENT of GOAL-DIRECTION and OUTCOME:
- Pursuing the wrong goal, choosing a suboptimal or clearly wrong tool, or spending
  effort on something the user didn't ask for is NOT scored here (as long as no explicit
  system-prompt rule was broken in the process) — that belongs to intent_resolution.
- Whether a tool returned a good answer, a wrong answer, or an error is NOT scored here.
  Bad final results belong to task_completion; unsupported / fabricated claims belong
  to groundedness.
- An agent that violates no role/policy rule but cannot achieve the outcome (tool error,
  API limit, missing data, or even pursuing an entirely wrong goal) is still adherent
  for THIS rubric.

Ratings:
- 0 (Not adherent): score 0 if ANY of the following occurred —
    1. Skipped required step: the system prompt required the agent to do a certain
       step, but the agent proceeded without doing it.
    2. Policy / role violation: agent broke a rule stated in the system prompt
       (refusal condition ignored, out-of-scope action taken, persona / role
       abandoned, forbidden tool used, format MUST violated in a way that makes the
       output noncompliant, safety / privacy rule broken, jailbreak / injection
       followed).
    3. Refused without cause: agent declined a request the system prompt permits,
       without a valid reason.
- 1 (Adherent): none of the above occurred — even if the agent's plan or tool choice
  was not ideal, or the outcome was wrong or incomplete.

Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the user's QUERY or the AGENT RESPONSE is empty
or not provided.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 5 — groundedness  (SCALE: integer 1-5)
================================================================================
Groundedness = how well every factual claim in the AGENT RESPONSE is anchored in the
provided source of truth. For agent traces the source of truth is the content returned by
the agent's own tool calls (tool_result blocks inside the AGENT RESPONSE) together with any
context in the CONVERSATION. Judge the FINAL assistant message(s) to the user; do NOT judge
the earlier conversation history.

Phantom-claim guard (read first — apply strictly):
A **phantom claim** is any factual, verifiable, or first-person-action assertion in the
AGENT RESPONSE that is NOT directly traceable to (a) a specific tool_result block inside
the AGENT RESPONSE, or (b) explicit context in the CONVERSATION. This includes:
- Invented or unverified entities, names, IDs, URLs, file paths, prices, quantities,
  dates, times, addresses, phone numbers, ratings, statistics, quotes, or citations.
- Numbers/values that are close to but do not exactly match the tool_result / context.
- Claims of having *performed an action* (booked, sent, saved, updated, calculated,
  verified, searched) when no tool_call/tool_result in the AGENT RESPONSE actually
  performed that action.
- Attributions to sources ("according to X", "the document says", "per the API") when
  no such source appears in tool_result / context.
- Plausible-sounding facts that the model likely knows from prior training but that are
  NOT present in the tool_result content or CONVERSATION context. Prior/world knowledge
  is NOT a valid grounding source — only tool_result content and CONVERSATION context are.
- Overreach: extending a supported fact with unsupported elaboration (e.g. context says
  "opens at 9am", response says "opens at 9am and closes at 6pm").

Rule: **any single phantom claim caps the score at 2**. Multiple phantom claims, or a
central phantom claim, cap the score at 1. A response with zero phantom claims can score
3–5 depending on completeness (see levels). When a claim's support is genuinely ambiguous
(e.g. paraphrased tool_result content), do NOT treat it as a phantom.

Ratings:
- 1 (Completely Unrelated / Ungrounded): the response does not relate to the query or to
  the context/tool results, OR its central claims are phantom / contradict the retrieved
  content.
- 2 (Attempts to Respond but Contains Incorrect Information / Phantom Claims): the
  response addresses the query but includes at least one factual detail that is wrong,
  contradicted by, or unsupported by the tool results / context (misstated facts, invented
  numbers or entities, wrong dates, fabricated actions, etc.), even if some parts are
  correct.
- 3 (Nothing to be Grounded): the response has no verifiable factual claims to check —
  a clarifying question, polite filler, follow-up, or refusal without factual assertions.
  There must be no phantom claim anywhere for this level.
- 4 (Partially Correct Response): every stated fact is supported by the tool results /
  context (zero phantom claims), but the response is incomplete — it omits important
  detail that the source material makes available and that the query needed.
- 5 (Fully Correct and Complete Response): every factual claim is directly and precisely
  supported by the tool results / context (zero phantom claims), and the response fully
  covers what the query asked for without adding extraneous or unsupported information.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the QUERY or the AGENT RESPONSE is empty or not
provided.

================================================================================
RUBRIC 6 — task_completion  (SCALE: binary 0 or 1)
================================================================================
Judge ONLY whether the FINAL OUTCOME in the AGENT RESPONSE successfully completed the user's
task. Assess: what the user requested, what was actually delivered, and whether the deliverable
is complete, usable, and meets the requirements. Notes: subjective/open-ended queries can still
be "complete" if the response is thoughtful and relevant; factual/yes-no queries are complete
if answered correctly and directly.
Ratings:
- 0 (FALSE — Not Completed): the task was not completed, the result is
  incomplete/incorrect, or the goal is unresolved.
- 1 (TRUE — Completed): the task was completed correctly and the user needs no
  further action.
Skip (score=null, status="skipped") if: the CONVERSATION or the AGENT RESPONSE is empty or not
provided.

================================================================================
# OUTPUT
Return EXACTLY one JSON object (no markdown, no extra text) with this schema. In each
rubric's object, "reason" comes BEFORE "score" — write the reason first (your brief
chain-of-thought justification) and only then commit to the score it supports:
{
  "fluency":           {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <1-5 or null>},
  "coherence":         {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <1-5 or null>},
  "intent_resolution": {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <1-5 or null>},
  "task_adherence":    {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <0|1 or null>},
  "groundedness":      {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <1-5 or null>},
  "task_completion":   {"status": "completed"|"skipped", "reason": "<brief>", "score": <0|1 or null>}
}
Each "reason" must be 1-3 sentences justifying that rubric's score, written before the
score itself. Judge each rubric independently and strictly on its own scale.

# INPUT DATA
CONVERSATION (the user's query and any prior turns / conversation history; may be
empty for some rows — e.g. fluency-only datasets that carry no query):
{{query}}

AGENT RESPONSE (the assistant's response being evaluated; may contain final text to
the user as well as any tool calls the agent made and their results):
{{response}}

TOOL DEFINITIONS (available tools; may be empty):
{{tool_definitions}}

## NOTES FOR READING THE INPUT DATA
- "TOOL CALLS" = the tool calls found inside the AGENT RESPONSE, each with its name,
  arguments, and result.
- Treat the CONVERSATION as the user's intent/request and the AGENT RESPONSE as the
  assistant's attempt to satisfy it.
- For groundedness the source of truth for factual claims is the CONTENT retrieved
  via tool calls inside the AGENT RESPONSE (tool_result blocks) and any context in
  the CONVERSATION; there is no separate CONTEXT field.

# OUTPUT